1
00:00:00,209 --> 00:00:05,326
Failure Forensics | Trace the mistake, not just the final answer
A real app walkthrough using an invented invoice and a deliberately injected error.

2
00:00:05,326 --> 00:00:12,266
01 / Start with a document
The source says 814 EUR. This synthetic example deliberately extracts 999 EUR.

3
00:00:12,266 --> 00:00:17,314
02 / A finished run can still contain a wrong answer
Processing status and content quality are kept separate. Findings remain hypotheses.

4
00:00:17,314 --> 00:00:23,399
03 / Follow the actual dependencies
Extraction and classification branch independently. The summary uses both and the source.

5
00:00:23,399 --> 00:00:29,926
04 / Compare evidence with the output
Source: 814 EUR. Extracted fact: 999 EUR. The suspected starting point is visible.

6
00:00:29,926 --> 00:00:34,644
05 / Human review stays in control
Confirm, correct or mark a finding as uncertain. No label is silently treated as ground truth.

7
00:00:34,644 --> 00:00:41,189
06 / Change one fact, then test the result
Correct 999 to 814 using a normal input. Keep the original run intact.

8
00:00:41,189 --> 00:00:47,737
07 / Inspect what changed
The replay updates affected steps. A changed result is evidence to investigate, not proof of causality.

9
00:00:47,737 --> 00:00:52,778
08 / Turn reviewed examples into repeatable evaluation
Save reviewed cases in versioned datasets. The demo measures mechanics, not real-model accuracy.

10
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:57,640
